  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Life, body and the living letter",
      "body": "<p>Tamil grammar names its letters after the body. The 12 vowels are <i>uyir</i>, 'life', and the 18 consonants are <i>mey</i>, 'body'. A consonant on its own is written with a dot, the <i>pulli</i>: <span lang=\"ta\">க்</span> is a bare k.</p>\n<p>Join a body to a life and you get an <i>uyirmey</i>, a 'living body': <span lang=\"ta\">க் + அ = க</span> ka, <span lang=\"ta\">க் + ஆ = கா</span> kā. With 18 × 12 combinations, there are 216 of these, but most are regular: the vowel signs ா ி ீ ெ ே ை ொ ோ ௌ look the same on every consonant.</p>\n<p>The exceptions are u and ū, whose signs fuse with the consonant in a few different ways (<span lang=\"ta\">கு மு து ரு</span>). Learn them in groups.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "One letter, two sounds",
      "body": "<p>Tamil does not write the difference between k and g, t and d, or p and b. The same letter covers both, and the sound depends on where it sits:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>at the start of a word, or doubled</td><td>voiceless: <span lang=\"ta\">பால்</span> pāl, <span lang=\"ta\">அப்பா</span> appā</td></tr>\n<tr><td>after a nasal</td><td>voiced: <span lang=\"ta\">பந்து</span> pandu (ball)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>between vowels</td><td>voiced or softened: <span lang=\"ta\">பகல்</span> pagal (daytime)</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Tamil also has no aspirated letters: there is no separate kh or bh, which makes the alphabet one of the smallest in India.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Three l's, three n's, two r's",
      "body": "<p>To make up for the small alphabet, Tamil splits some sounds English treats as one.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"ta\">ல ள ழ</td><td>plain l, curled-back l, and the zh-like ḻ of <span lang=\"ta\">தமிழ்</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"ta\">ந ன ண</td><td>dental n (start of a word), gum-ridge n (end of a word), curled-back n</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"ta\">ர ற</td><td>a light tap and a strong trill</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>ழ is hard for many learners and for some native speakers, who say it like ள. Aim for the tongue curled back without touching the roof of the mouth.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Tamil numbers and old symbols",
      "body": "<p>Tamil has its own digits (<span lang=\"ta\">௧ ௨ ௩</span> for 1 2 3), but today people use the international digits almost everywhere. You may still see the old ones on clocks, temple walls and calendars.</p>"
    }
  ]
